{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEven with schema-filtered selection (the selection story this depends on), extraction from\nfree text stays heuristic: a trailing schema-valid decoy can win, and models fence-wrap quoted\nJSON so fences cannot serve as the channel (measured: E2 catch 2, experiment-results comment\non the selection bug). An explicit output channel makes the payload location a stated contract\ninstead of an inference.\n\n## Approach\n\nExtend `schema_directive` (src/rebar/llm/structured.py:75) to instruct the model to wrap its\nsingle final JSON object between the literal markers `<<<REBAR_OUTPUT>>>` and `<<<END>>>`.\n\nExtraction is added to `parse_structured` (src/rebar/llm/structured.py:301) as a THIN\nfront-narrowing step, NOT a parallel validate path: when the reply contains one or more COMPLETE\nmarker blocks (a `<<<REBAR_OUTPUT>>>` followed by a later `<<<END>>>`), the existing\nselection-then-validate pipeline (`_candidate_dicts` -> `validate_to` (:189) -> last-valid-wins\n-> tolerant_parse) is run over the CONCATENATED marker-region text (in document order, so the\nLAST block's object is naturally the last candidate and wins) INSTEAD of the full reply. When\nthere are ZERO complete marker blocks, the pipeline runs over the full reply exactly as today.\nThis design deliberately reuses df3a's semantics verbatim; the only new behavior is restricting\nthe scanned text to marker regions when the model used the channel.\n\nFailure/fall-through rule (mirrors df3a, made explicit — this is the boundary the review flagged):\n\n- Markers PRESENT (>= 1 complete block), a candidate validates: return the LAST validated\n  object (df3a last-wins), scoped to marker regions.\n- Markers PRESENT, content needs json-repair (trailing comma, unclosed brace, smart quotes,\n  fence-wrapped object inside the block): it flows through the SAME `tolerant_parse` recovery\n  df3a applies, scoped to the marker region — so repair behavior is inherited, not reinvented.\n- Markers PRESENT but NOTHING in the marker regions validates even after repair: RAISE the last\n  candidate's validation error (df3a's \"raise the LAST one's error\", fail-closed). It does NOT\n  fall through to a full-reply selection — a reply that USED the channel but produced invalid\n  content must fail-closed rather than let a decoy OUTSIDE the markers win. This is the single\n  bounded-retry signal (layer 4), identical to df3a.\n- Markers ABSENT, or an opening `<<<REBAR_OUTPUT>>>` with NO closing `<<<END>>>` (an incomplete\n  block is treated as no complete block): fall through byte-for-byte to today's full-reply\n  selection pipeline, so df3a-era and non-compliant-model transcripts are unaffected.\n\nThe directive literal is SINGLE-SOURCED as a module constant in structured.py (e.g.\n`SENTINEL_DIRECTIVE`, naming both markers) so exactly one string is used by `schema_directive`,\nby the marker extractor, and by the measurement harness — no drift between what the model is\ntold and what the parser looks for. The append site at src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py:211-212\n(`schema_hint = structured.schema_directive(model_cls)`; also re-appended on the bounded retry at\n:237) already carries `schema_directive`'s output, so single-sourcing the constant means the\nretry path inherits the markers too; that call site does not change.\n\nInstruction-locality reconciliation (review finding): `schema_directive`'s existing literal says\n\"ONLY a single JSON object ... no prose, no markdown fence\". The marker instruction does NOT\ncontradict it — `<<<REBAR_OUTPUT>>>`/`<<<END>>>` are literal SENTINEL lines, not prose and not a\nmarkdown code fence. The single-sourced constant states this explicitly: still exactly one JSON\nobject, still no prose and no ``` fence; ADDITIONALLY, place that one object between the two\nliteral marker lines. So one prompt string carries a single, non-conflicting instruction.\n\nDirective and extraction land in ONE change, deliberately: E2 measured that shipping the\ndirective alone flips quote-in-prose from 0% to 100% and breaks the current parser 10/10 — a\nsplit would leave the system broken between changes. The marker literal is the E2-tested one\n(16/16 channel compliance across sonnet/haiku on Bedrock, 0/26 decoy wins). Prior art:\nAnthropic's answer-tag extraction guidance and aider's `<<<`-style sentinel blocks; markdown\nfences are disqualified as the channel because models fence-wrap quoted material (E2).\n\nThe a40f harness currently appends a PLACEHOLDER directive\n(tests/external/test_structured_output_matrix.py `_SENTINEL_DIRECTIVE`, whose own comment says\n\"the sentinel story (a sibling) owns refining it\"); this story replaces that placeholder with the\nreal single-sourced constant so the operator-attested before/after run (AC5) measures THIS\nstory's actual directive against the current parser, not a prototype.\n\n## Scope\n\n- src/rebar/llm/structured.py — the single-sourced `SENTINEL_DIRECTIVE` constant (with the\n  reconciled \"single object, no prose/fence, wrapped between the two marker lines\" wording), its\n  inclusion in `schema_directive` (:75), and the marker-region front-narrowing in\n  `parse_structured` (:301): complete blocks -> run today's pipeline over the marker regions;\n  no complete block -> run it over the full reply unchanged\n- tests/external/test_structured_output_matrix.py — replace the placeholder `_SENTINEL_DIRECTIVE`\n  with the single-sourced constant so the harness measures this story's real directive\n- tests/unit/test_structured.py — marker pins, incl. the fenced-quote hazard pin and the\n  malformed/incomplete-marker pins below\n- tests/fixtures/ — E2 sentinel replies added to the corpus\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit/contract tier (real reply text through the real parser):\n\n- Marker extraction pins: one block; multiple blocks -> LAST wins; markerless -> selection\n  fallback yields the identical result (parameterized contrast against the selection story's\n  tests); marker block containing a fenced object -> payload still wins.\n- The fenced-quote hazard pin: a fence-wrapped schema-decoy plus a marker-wrapped payload ->\n  the marker payload wins (regression pin for E2 catch 2).\n- Marker-first ordering pin: a reply carrying BOTH a marker-wrapped valid payload AND an\n  earlier schema-valid decoy object parses to the MARKER payload (markers beat the selection\n  pipeline). Mutation check: dropping marker priority (extraction ignores markers, or runs the\n  selection pipeline over the full reply first) turns this and the E2-reply corpus test RED.\n- Malformed/incomplete-marker pins (the review-flagged boundary):\n  - An opening `<<<REBAR_OUTPUT>>>` with NO closing `<<<END>>>` -> treated as markerless: the\n    full-reply selection pipeline runs and yields today's result.\n  - A marker block whose content needs json-repair (trailing comma / unclosed brace) ->\n    repaired via the inherited tolerant_parse path, scoped to the marker region.\n  - A marker block whose content is schema-INVALID with a valid schema-decoy OUTSIDE the markers\n    -> RAISES StructuredOutputError (fail-closed); it does NOT fall through and pick the outside\n    decoy. Mutation check: making the invalid-marker case fall through to full-reply selection\n    turns this RED.\n- Markerless regression pin: a reply with NO markers yields the IDENTICAL result the\n  schema-filtered selection pipeline produces today (byte-for-byte fall-through), so df3a-era and\n  non-compliant-model transcripts are unaffected.\n- LLM-surface evidence is measured by the harness (test-design §10: paired directives, fixed n,\n  deterministic scoring) and recorded here as an operator-attested artifact (AC5).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The directive names both markers, reconciled with the \"single object, no prose/fence\"\n      clause (markers are sentinels, not a fence), and extraction honors them with\n      last-block-wins — checked by the new unit pins in tests/unit/test_structured.py.\n- [ ] A markerless reply, AND an unterminated opening marker with no close, both fall back to\n      schema-filtered full-reply selection with identical results — checked by parameterized\n      contrast/boundary tests.\n- [ ] A fence-wrapped decoy alongside a marker-wrapped payload parses to the payload — checked\n      by the pinned hazard test.\n- [ ] A reply carrying a marker-wrapped valid payload AND an EARLIER plain schema-valid decoy\n      object parses to the marker payload; and a reply whose marker block is schema-INVALID\n      while a valid decoy sits OUTSIDE the markers RAISES (fail-closed) rather than selecting the\n      outside decoy — checked by the marker-first ordering + malformed-marker pins in\n      tests/unit/test_structured.py.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] A harness before/after run (old vs sentinel directive) records, PER\n      CELL, three separate measurements — reported honestly, not collapsed into one bar:\n      (a) marker COMPLIANCE (share of replies wrapping the payload in the markers) at or above\n          the predeclared channel-adoption threshold (>= 95% per cell; E2 measured 16/16);\n      (b) NO DECOY WINS on marker-compliant replies (extraction picks the marked payload, 0\n          decoy wins), and\n      (c) overall PARSE SUCCESS does not REGRESS versus the a40f-inherited baseline per cell,\n          and improves on cells whose failures were location/decoy-driven.\n      This story provides a reliable output CHANNEL; absolute parse success is bounded above by\n      CONTENT validity (a model emitting wrong keys/values), which a channel does not fix — the\n      a40f run already measured ~55% parse success on anthropic/bedrock under a placeholder\n      sentinel directive with layout already 100% prose, so those residual failures are content,\n      not channel. Gating this story on an absolute >= 95% parse-success bar would mis-measure\n      channel-compliance as a proxy for content-validity; if compliance/no-decoy-win pass but\n      absolute parse success stays below 95% because of content failures, that is a PASS for this\n      story and any residual content gap is recorded as discovered work, not a block. The\n      artifact link is recorded as a comment on this ticket."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785919509672790001,"uuid":"749d510c-c056-4152-a3bb-47b95871660c"}